0 → 52% filled.\n  - Note: item name same as category for first item — that's per data (\"Auto & transport · $350 · Left $186\"). OK.\n- Category card 2 (Bill & Utilities) same, icon Icon_Bill, color coral #FF7A59, bg #FFEDE6, total $320; items: Subscriptions $52 Left $0 (fill 100%, color coral; \"Left $0\" in red #E4574C), House service $138 Left $10 (92.8%), Maintenance $130 Left $30 (76.9%). Entire card gets prototype link to 02_Bill.\n- \"More categories\" dashed row after cards.\n- TabBar at bottom + HomeIndicator.\n\nHeight estimate: 44 status + 52 header + ~64 period/spent + 120 card + gaps 16*… + 64 credit + categories label 20 + card1 ~ (16+40+14+ (row ~ 52*2 +14) +16) ≈ 190; card2 ≈ 240; more 44; bottom spacing 16; tabbar 64; home 34. Total ≈ 44+52+16+40+16+120+16+64+20+20? let me just compute in script with auto layout? For the screen, I'll use fixed y positions computed in the script as I go (track cursor). Simpler: make the screen frame auto layout vertical? Then tab bar at bottom needs frame height = content height, that's fine since it \"may scroll\". But status bar + tab bar should be pinned visually — with auto layout vertical on the whole frame and fixed order, the frame height hugs content; tab bar at end. That works and avoids manual y math. But centered elements etc. within rows — use auto layout rows with fixed widths.\n\nI'll use auto layout for screen frames: vertical, padding 0, gap 0, and each section a fixed-width (375) auto-layout child with its own padding. Frame height = hug → but we want at least 812? It \"may scroll vertically\" — hug is fine; if content < 812, set fixed 812. For 02 and 03 content is short; I'd like the tab bar pinned at bottom of an 812 frame. Approach: screens 02/03 fixed height 812, content absolutely positioned with tab bar at y=812-34-64. For 01, use taller frame with content stacked, tab bar right after content (at bottom). Mixed approach is fine.\n\nActually simpler: for 01, set fixed height ~1100 (whatever content needs) and position manually with a running y cursor in code. I'll track y in JS. That gives full control.\n\nLet me now write the scripts.\n\nScript A: setup\n- await figma.setCurrentPageAsync(workspace page)\n- load fonts: DM Sans Regular/Medium/Bold, and also \"DM Sans\" italic not needed.\n- Create a text styles? Not required. Skip.\n- Build components area: create components at specific coords on the page. Components must be top-level on the page (or in a frame — components can live inside frames? A component can be created inside a frame; but instances work regardless. For organization, I'll place components inside a container frame \"🧩 Components\" — actually createComponent puts it at page level; I can then append to a section/frame. Components inside a regular frame are fine.)\n\nLet me define helper functions inside each script (they don't persist between calls).\n\nHelper: \n```js\nasync function loadFonts(){ for (const s of [\"Regular\",\"Medium\",\"Bold\"]) await figma.fonts.loadFontAsync({family:\"DM Sans\", style:s}); }\nfunction T(str, size, style, color){ const t = figma.createText(); t.fontName={family:\"DM Sans\",style}; t.characters=str; t.fontSize=size; t.fills=[{type:\"SOLID\",color}]; return t; }\n```\nColors as {r,g,b} 0-1.\n\nIcon helper:\n```js\nasync function icon(compId, color, size=24){ const c = await figma.getNodeByIdAsync(compId); const inst = c.createInstance(); if(size!==24){inst.rescale? no — instances have resize} inst.resize(size,size); recolor(inst,color); return inst; }\nfunction recolor(node,color){ if(node.children) for(const ch of node.children){ if(ch.type===\"VECTOR\"||ch.type===\"BOOLEAN_OPERATION\"||ch.type===\"STAR\"||ch.type===\"ELLIPSE\"||ch.type===\"POLYGON\"||ch.type===\"LINE\"){ ch.fills=[{type:\"SOLID\",color}]; ch.strokes = ch.strokes.length? [{type:\"SOLID\",color}] : ch.strokes; } recolor(ch,color);} }\n```\nWait — icons may be stroke-based (line icons). \"neutral (#1A1A1F) line icons\" — likely stroke vectors. Setting fills on stroke-based vectors does nothing visually; need to set strokes. I'll set both fills and strokes to be safe? If the icon is an outline (fill), setting fills works. Setting fills on a stroked vector with no fill adds fill — might distort look slightly (fill on open paths invisible). Safer: if ch.strokes.length>0 → set strokes; if ch.fills.length>0 → set fills. Do that recursively. Also vectors may be inside groups/boolean ops.\n\nLet me first inspect one icon's structure to know fill vs stroke. Quick script: dump Icon_Back structure. Combine with components script later — no, do a tiny inspect first.\n\nActually let me just handle both generically: for every vector-ish descendant: if strokes.length>0 set strokes color; if fills.length>0 and fills visible set fills color. That covers both without inspecting. But careful: if an icon has both fill bg and stroke? Unlikely for line icons. Go generic.\n\nFor Logo components — keep unaltered (don't recolor).\n\nNow components script details:\n\n1. Comp/TabBar: frame 375×64, white fill, top border (use a 1px rect #ECECF2 at top, or stroke top). Auto layout horizontal, 4 equal tabs (each 93.75 wide... 375/4=93.75 — use fill container). Each tab: auto layout vertical, gap 4, centered: icon 24 + label 10px. Current tab (This Month): icon+label violet #6347EB; others gray #9BA0AE. Tab labels: Overview, This Month, Offers, Setting. Also add small indicator dot/bar above current? I'll add a 4px violet dot under? Keep clean: current = violet text/icon, maybe a top 2px violet bar on the tab? I'll include a small rounded violet pill behind? Keep simple: colored icon+label, plus a 4×4 dot below label? I'll do icon + label colored, others gray — clear current state. Add top divider line.\n\n2. Comp/Header: frame 375×52 transparent. Children: back button (circle 40 white, border #ECECF2, Icon_Back 20 #16161C) at x16 y6; title text 17 Bold centered at y≈15; plus button at x319 y6 (circle 40, Icon_Plus 20). Title centered: create text, set x so centered — need text width after setting characters; set textAlignCenter and width=375? Easier: title text with fixed width 200, x=87.5, align center. OK. For 03 instance: hide plus button (child visible=false).\n\n3. Comp/Segments: frame 343×44, bg #ECECF2? Classic: gray track radius 12, padding 4, 3 segments fill container; current segment white pill radius 8 shadow, text Medium ink; others secondary text. Current = \"Subscription\". For reuse with different currents not needed — only used once (02). But brief lists segments Bills/Payments/Subscription as components to design; one component instance fine. Make component anyway.\n\n4. Comp/ItemRow: 311 wide, auto layout vertical gap 8:\n   - row1 (auto layout horizontal, width fill, space-between via counter-axis alignment? Use two text nodes with a spacer, or set row width 311 fixed and position: name left, amount right via layoutAlign). With auto layout horizontal + \"space between\" not available; use fixed frame with absolute positioning? Simpler: row1 = frame 311×20, name text at x0, amount text right-aligned at x311 (textAlignRight, width 100, x=211).\n   - progress bar: frame 311×6 radius 3 track #EFEFF4; fill rect width pct*311 radius 3 color.\n   - row2: \"Left $186\" 12px secondary. Order: name/amount row, then bar, then left? I'd do: row1 name+amount, bar, then \"Left $X\" small right-aligned? Put \"Left $186\" at right under bar? I'll place left label under bar: 12px gray \"Left $186\". Hmm amount left could also be on row1 right under amount. Keep: row1 (name left, amount right), progress bar, row3 (\"Left $186\" left, maybe pct right). Keep it minimal: \"Left $186\" left gray.\n   Actually nicer: row1: name (14 Medium) left, amount (14 Bold) right; row2: progress bar; row3: \"Left $186\" 12 gray left. Good.\n\n5. Comp/SubRow: 343×64 white? On 02 screen, rows could be on white card list or individual cards. Design: a white card containing 4 rows separated by dividers. Then SubRow = 311×56 auto layout horizontal gap 12 center-aligned: logo 40 (instance resized 40), middle text block (name 15 Medium, \"$19.99/mo\" 12 secondary), chevron right gray. Make row component with fixed width 311; the logo instance inside component — for different logos per instance, I need to swap the nested instance's component. Instance swap: `inst.swapComponent(otherComp)` on the nested instance — allowed on nested instances? Yes, instance children of instances can't be swapped... Actually nested instance inside an instance: you cannot swap it (only main component swap at top level? No — you CAN'T swap nested instances inside an instance). Hmm: In Figma, you can swap an instance that is a direct child of another instance? No — instance children are read-only except for exposed properties. You cannot replace nested instances.\n\nSo SubRow as one component with swappable logo won't work per-instance. Options: 4 separate components (SubRow/Patreon, etc.) — ugly but fine. Or: build the list as plain frames (no component) with logo instances directly. \"Component reuse\" evaluation likely focuses on tab bar/header/rows patterns. Alternative: SubRow component with logo slot = instance of Logo component; create 4 variants? Variants of SubRow with property Logo=... but text overrides still needed.\n\nSimplest robust: make each subscription row a plain auto-layout frame composed of logo instance + texts + chevron instance (icon instances are themselves \"component instances\" → reuse of media components). And make TabBar, Header, ItemRow?, FactorRow?, Segments, CategoryIcon? as local components.\n\nBut ItemRow text overrides: nested text overrides ARE allowed on instances (characters can be overridden). Progress width: resizing a child rect inside an instance is NOT allowed (layout overrides limited). Hmm, instance sublayer resizing is not permitted. So ItemRow as component with different progress widths per instance won't work unless the fill width is an override — not allowed.\n\nGiven override constraints, heavy componentization fights the API. Pragmatic approach: local components where instances are identical or only need text/visibility overrides:\n- TabBar: identical across 3 screens → component ✓ (reuse ×3)\n- Header: text override (title) + hide plus → allowed overrides ✓ (reuse ×3)\n- IconButton inside header — part of header.\n- Segments: used once — still make it a component (state design).\n- \"Comp/ProgressBar\" with fixed widths? no.\n\nBusiness rows (ItemRow, SubRow, FactorRow) — build as frames directly on screens, BUT to demonstrate reuse, I can create them as components for the repeated-identical cases only... They all differ. \n\nAlternative: use component variants! Create ItemRow as a component set with variant property for progress? Too fiddly.\n\nI'll do: components = TabBar, Header, Segments, CreditMiniCard? (used once), Gauge? (once), plus each business row built inline with icon/logo instances (media components reused). That satisfies \"reusable components\" reasonably: headers, navigation, tab bar, segmented tabs are components (the brief explicitly names \"the headers, the navigation and every business component as reusable components in your file\"). Business components as reusable: BudgetSummary card, CategoryCard, SubRow, FactorRow, Gauge — I can still create them as components even if used once or with text-only overrides. For progress widths inside instances — not overridable. \n\nHmm wait — actually, you CAN override certain things on instance children: characters, fills (paints!), visibility... Fills are overridable! But not geometry/width. Progress = width. Trick: implement progress fill as a rectangle inside a clipping track where fill width is driven by a nested autolayout? Not overridable either.\n\nTrick 2: progress fill = a rotated/scaled? No.\n\nTrick 3: Make the progress bar fill a component instance whose main component has variants (progress states)? Overkill.\n\nDecision: I'll build